\title{[GCD3] Worst-case GCD input (3 pts)}
\date{2026-09-01T17:22:31Z}
\tag{algorithms}
\tag{problem}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{In fact, more is true: [consecutive Fibonacci numbers](00JZ) #{(F_{n+1},
F_n)} are in some sense a \em{worst-case} input for the Euclidean
Algorithm: they are the \em{smallest} numbers for which the Euclidean
Algorithm needs #{n} steps.}

\p{Let #{S(n)} be the following proposition:}

\p{#{S(n) =} \em{For all natural numbers #{a} and #{b}, if #{a > b} and #{\mathsf{GCDRec}(a,b)} makes #{n} recursive calls, then #{a \geq F_{n+1}} and #{b \geq F_n}.}}

\p{That is, if #{R(a,b,n)} represents the proposition "#{\mathsf{GCDRec}(a,b)} makes #{n} recursive calls", then ##{S(n) = \forall a,b : \mathbb{N}.\; ((a > b) \land R(a,b,n)) \to ((a \geq F_{n+1}) \land (b \geq F_n))}.}

\ol{
  \li{Prove #{\forall n : \mathbb{N}. S(n)} by induction on #{n}:
  \ol{
    \li{What does the base case #{S(0)} say? Prove it.
    \hint{Look at the code for \GCDR.  What can you conclude about #{a} and #{b} if it makes zero recursive calls?}
    }
    \li{Let #{k} be an arbitrary natural number, and prove #{S(k) \to S(k+1)} by supposing #{S(k)} to be true and proving #{S(k+1)}. You may want to write out #{S(k)} and #{S(k+1)} in full to help you [keep track of what you know and what you are trying to prove](0057).
    \hint{Show that #{a \geq b + a \mod b}.}
    }
  }
  }

  \li{Explain why the previous result shows that if #{a \leq F_{n+1}}
  then the Euclidean Algorithm requires at most #{n} steps.}
}

